            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Ask four questions before discussing price: how they will prepare the slab, whether they moisture test first, what product and solids content they are installing, and exactly what the warranty covers. The preparation answer alone sorts most companies, because grinding is the reliable method and acid etching fails invisibly.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Preparation is also where a cheap quote finds its saving, since it is the largest labour cost and the one thing you cannot inspect once the coating is down. Normalise every quote on prep method, crack repair, moisture testing, coat count, topcoat and film thickness before comparing totals.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then check the company rather than the brochure. Ask for addresses of jobs three or more years old and go and look, because every fresh coating looks immaculate and only an older one shows whether it is still bonded. And treat a moisture exclusion in the warranty, combined with no moisture test, as the clearest possible signal that the guarantee is decorative.
          </p>
        </div>


